@page "/utility"

<CatHead Title="Utilities">
    Single-purpose classes, in <code>@@layer sedna.utilities</code>. Not a utility framework — the point
    of tier 2 is semantic classes, and a page assembled out of fifteen utilities has moved its design
    decisions into the markup where nobody reviews them.
</CatHead>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-stack-line"></i>
    <strong>The cascade layer is what makes these work.</strong> A utility is <code>(0,1,0)</code>
    and a component rule like <code>.table th, .table td</code> is <code>(0,1,1)</code>, so a utility
    loses on specificity alone. <code>sedna.utilities</code> sits after <code>sedna.paint</code>, so
    <code>.text-end</code> on a table cell wins regardless of it.
</div>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-forbid-line"></i>
    <strong>What is deliberately not here.</strong> No numeric spacing scale, no colour utilities —
    that is what tokens are for — no display utilities, because <code>.sedna-row</code> and
    <code>.sedna-col</code> already say it with meaning, and no breakpoint prefixes, because the library
    has no breakpoint vocabulary and should not gain one.
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Utility.Layout" Title="Rows and columns" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-row</code>, <code>.sedna-row-wrap</code> and <code>.sedna-col</code> are the arrangements
        that would otherwise be an inline <code>style="display:flex; gap:8px"</code> — which is most
        of them. <code>.sedna-push</code> pushes everything after it to the far end.
    </p>
    <p>
        These carry the <code>sedna-</code> prefix because they are library-owned utilities in a shared
        namespace, where <code>.row</code> and <code>.col</code> are names an app is very likely to
        have already.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Utility.Gaps" Title="Gaps" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-row</code> and <code>.sedna-col</code> carry one gap. These four override it:
        <code>.sedna-gap-0</code> removes it, and <code>1</code>, <code>2</code> and <code>3</code> are
        8px, 12px and 20px from the spacing scale.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Utility.Alignment" Title="Alignment" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-row</code> centres on the cross axis. <code>.sedna-start</code> is what a row of fields
        with labels wants; <code>.sedna-baseline</code> is what a row of text at different sizes wants.
        <code>.sedna-between</code> pushes the ends apart, and <code>.sedna-wrap</code> allows a second
        line.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Utility.Sizing" Title="Sizing and spacing" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-fill</code> is the flex child that takes the remaining room, and it includes
        <code>min-width: 0</code> — without which the child refuses to shrink below its content and
        every ellipsis inside it stops working. <code>.sedna-shrink-0</code> is the opposite, for the
        avatar and the badge that must not compress.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-mt-*</code> and <code>.sedna-mb-*</code> are block margins only, in the same four
        steps. There is no inline pair: horizontal placement belongs to the layout, not to a utility
        on the child. <code>.sedna-scroll-x</code> creates a horizontal scroller —
        <code>.sedna-scroll</code> styles one, which is a different job.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Utility.Text" Title="Text" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The alignment utilities are logical (<code>start</code> / <code>end</code>), so they mirror
        with the document direction like everything else in the library.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.text-truncate</code> is one line with an ellipsis and includes
        <code>min-width: 0</code> for the same reason as <code>.sedna-fill</code>.
        <code>.text-clamp-2</code> and <code>-3</code> cut after that many lines.
        <code>.text-break</code> breaks inside a word when there is nowhere else — an id, a URL, a
        path in a narrow column.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.text-sm</code> and <code>.text-lg</code> are one type step either side of the body
        size, for a line that has to be quieter or louder without becoming a heading.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Utility.HiddenButNotGone" Title="Hidden, but not gone" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.visually-hidden</code> takes text out of the rendering and leaves it in the
        accessibility tree — a name for an icon-only control, a table caption, the target of an
        <code>aria-describedby</code>. Add <code>--focusable</code> and it comes back when reached by
        keyboard, which is how a skip link works.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Utility.StateAndPrint" Title="State, dividers and print" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-busy</code> dims a block and stops interaction while it reloads. Pair it with
        <code>aria-busy="true"</code>, which is the part that is announced.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-invisible</code> is <em>not</em> a replacement for the <code>hidden</code>
        attribute: use the attribute, which also removes the element from the accessibility tree and
        the tab order. This is only for hiding something that must keep its space.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-disabled</code> is for something unavailable that is <strong>not</strong> a
        <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> — those have
        the <code>disabled</code> attribute, which is announced and removes them from the tab order,
        and this is no substitute for it. Add <code>aria-disabled="true"</code> alongside.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-divider-top</code> and <code>-bottom</code> are a hairline between rows without an
        <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>. <code>.sedna-no-print</code> and <code>.sedna-print-only</code> are the two
        every printed page needs.
    </p>
</CatExample>
